course: dictionary
lesson: 15530
title: Words that show order
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you the order things happen in. Successively is one of them. It means one after another, in order, without a break. «The runners crossed the finish line successively, one every ten seconds.» Each runner follows the one before, and nobody is skipped.
explain: Successively is a formal word. People often say «in turn» or «one after another» instead, and these mean nearly the same thing. The clues are in the sentence: the word tells you there is a list, and the list goes in an order. «The numbers grew successively larger: 2, 4, 8, 16.» Each number is bigger than the one before it.
explain: Use the swap check. Put «one after another» in place of successively. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning. «The kings ruled successively.» Swapped, it reads «The kings ruled one after another», and that is exactly what it means.
fact: Successively means one after another, in order, without a break.
fact: «Successively larger» means each one is bigger than the one before.
fact: «Successively smaller» means each one is smaller than the one before.
fact: «The water was poured successively into three cups.» means one cup was filled, then the next, then the next.
fact: Successively is an adverb, so it tells you how something happens. Other words with a similar meaning are in turn and one after another.
exercise: «The lights came on successively, one after another.» What does successively mean here? | Very brightly | All at the same time | Not at all | *In order, one after another
exercise: «The numbers grew successively larger: 3, 9, 27, 81.» Which words could replace successively? | Unfortunately and sadly | Suddenly and loudly | *Each time, one after another | Quietly and slowly
exercise: In science, you add the powder successively, one spoon at a time. What does this tell you to do? | *Add one spoon, then the next, in order | Pour it all in at once | Skip every second spoon | Stir it before you start
test: «The kings ruled successively.» What does this sentence tell you? | All the kings ruled at the same time | The kings never ruled at all | *One king ruled, then the next, in order | Only one king ever ruled
test: Which phrase means numbers that get smaller each time? | *Successively smaller | Successively larger | Successively sleepy | Successively noisy
exam: «The pupils lined up successively.» Which swap keeps the meaning? | Backwards | Suddenly | Quietly | *One after another
